WEBVTT

0
00:00:00.289 --> 00:00:00.600
Today,

1
00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:02.789
we have discussed how Stanford approaches

2
00:00:02.930 --> 00:00:04.960
external market competitiveness,

3
00:00:05.289 --> 00:00:06.869
internal comparability

4
00:00:07.079 --> 00:00:08.649
and performance recognition

5
00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:10.989
as part of our compensation philosophy

6
00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:15.829
and how you as a manager can work with your HR partners to balance these factors

7
00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:18.200
when considering compensation for your teams.

8
00:00:18.370 --> 00:00:19.049
Now it's time to bring.

9
00:00:19.165 --> 00:00:21.405
it all together and discuss how to approach pay

10
00:00:21.405 --> 00:00:24.684
transparency and compensation conversations with your direct reports.

11
00:00:24.844 --> 00:00:26.405
Let's quickly refresh ourselves on the

12
00:00:26.405 --> 00:00:28.864
factors that go into individual pay decisions

13
00:00:29.045 --> 00:00:31.805
and how they align with the broader principles discussed today.

14
00:00:31.965 --> 00:00:32.264
First,

15
00:00:32.685 --> 00:00:35.064
we discussed external market competitiveness

16
00:00:35.165 --> 00:00:38.145
as a core principle of Stanford's compensation philosophy.

17
00:00:38.849 --> 00:00:42.830
This takes the shape of robust market benchmarking conducted annually.

18
00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:45.029
This informs the role specific

19
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:48.750
market-based pay range that is assigned to each job at Stanford.

20
00:00:48.889 --> 00:00:52.189
This provides the guardrails for individual compensation decisions

21
00:00:52.409 --> 00:00:56.430
and is further informed by each school and unit's business practices and budgets.

22
00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:57.389
Next,

23
00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:00.869
we talked about work location as a key factor of compensation.

24
00:01:01.119 --> 00:01:03.779
This spans both external market competitiveness

25
00:01:04.199 --> 00:01:05.620
and internal comparability.

26
00:01:05.839 --> 00:01:10.400
Stanford uses a location-based pay model and adjusts role-specific market-based

27
00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:13.269
pay ranges based on the location where work is performed.

28
00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:14.400
There are 5 locations.

29
00:01:14.535 --> 00:01:17.745
Based pay zones and market-based pay ranges are adjusted to

30
00:01:17.745 --> 00:01:20.084
reflect the cost of labor in each of these zones.

31
00:01:20.334 --> 00:01:22.785
Stanford's location-based pay approach is also used

32
00:01:22.785 --> 00:01:25.485
to support internal comparability and pay equity

33
00:01:25.665 --> 00:01:28.375
by ensuring that work location is taken into account when

34
00:01:28.375 --> 00:01:31.535
making pay decisions for employees working in similar jobs.

35
00:01:31.705 --> 00:01:32.044
Then,

36
00:01:32.584 --> 00:01:34.525
we took a look at internal comparability

37
00:01:34.665 --> 00:01:37.904
and how Stanford defines similar roles using our job structure.

38
00:01:38.224 --> 00:01:40.785
Similar jobs share the same job family,

39
00:01:41.184 --> 00:01:42.214
job function,

40
00:01:42.544 --> 00:01:43.464
and job level.

41
00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:46.319
And market-based pay ranges for those jobs

42
00:01:46.319 --> 00:01:48.510
are determined based on the job description,

43
00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:50.440
which includes expectations for job

44
00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:52.959
responsibilities and the required knowledge,

45
00:01:53.319 --> 00:01:53.910
skills

46
00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.580
and experience for the job.

47
00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:57.339
Employees in similar jobs

48
00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:58.680
with similar knowledge,

49
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:00.419
skills and experience

50
00:02:00.599 --> 00:02:02.239
should be compensated similarly.

51
00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:05.680
Compensation starts to vary when there are differences

52
00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:08.809
in these areas between employees in similar jobs.

53
00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:09.529
Finally,

54
00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:11.639
we discussed performance recognition,

55
00:02:11.889 --> 00:02:15.470
which is also a key component of Stanford's compensation philosophy

56
00:02:15.649 --> 00:02:17.550
and is linked to internal comparability.

57
00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:19.610
Stanford is committed to providing a

58
00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:22.470
fair and competitive staff compensation program

59
00:02:22.649 --> 00:02:23.570
that will attract,

60
00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:24.550
retain

61
00:02:24.729 --> 00:02:27.960
and reward high performing employees at all levels.

62
00:02:28.089 --> 00:02:31.369
Performance review data is considered as part of compensation.

63
00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:35.899
And this enables performance informed decision about merit-based rewards.

64
00:02:36.149 --> 00:02:36.809
Remember,

65
00:02:37.149 --> 00:02:40.309
these principles are ones to understand at a basic level,

66
00:02:40.589 --> 00:02:44.449
as they will help you when discussing compensation with your direct reports.

67
00:02:44.589 --> 00:02:48.339
Your local HR partner will support you in navigating each of these areas,

68
00:02:48.589 --> 00:02:50.589
so remember to always consult with them for

69
00:02:50.589 --> 00:02:52.630
anything related to compensation for your team.